## How to Use

{% if intrinsic_layout %}
To use this intrinsic adapter in your application, you need to compose an adapter
(a factory function returning it, rather than a dedicated shim class) and an intrinsic
component. The adapter connects to the HuggingFace model repository
where the trained weights are stored, and the intrinsic wraps it as a composable Mellea
component that can be used with any compatible backend.

> **Repo layout:** the code below only works if `{{ userid }}/{{ intrinsic_name }}`
> was uploaded with `m alora upload ... --intrinsic --io-yaml ./io.yaml` — that
> command places weights at `{{ intrinsic_name }}/<base-model>/{{ adapter_type }}/`, which is
> where `LocalFileBinding` looks for them. The plain `m alora upload` (without
> `--intrinsic`) pushes weights to the repo root instead, and this code will
> fail with `ValueError: Intrinsic '{{ intrinsic_name }}' ... not found`.

Make sure you have Mellea installed (`uv pip install mellea`), then use the code below to
integrate this intrinsic into your application.

```python
import mellea.stdlib.functional as mfuncs
from mellea.core import Context
from mellea.backends import Backend
from mellea.stdlib.components.simple import SimpleComponent
from mellea.backends.adapters import AdapterMixin
from mellea.backends.adapters import Adapter, Identity, LocalFileBinding, get_io_contract
from mellea.backends.adapters.catalog import AdapterType
from mellea.stdlib.components.intrinsic import Intrinsic


_INTRINSIC_MODEL_ID = "{{ userid }}/{{ intrinsic_name }}"
_INTRINSIC_ADAPTER_NAME = "{{ intrinsic_name }}"
_INTRINSIC_QUALIFIED_NAME = f"{_INTRINSIC_ADAPTER_NAME}_{AdapterType.{{ adapter_type | upper }}.value}"


def _{{ intrinsic_name }}_adapter() -> Adapter:
    """Compose this trained adapter (LoRA/aLoRA weights at `_INTRINSIC_MODEL_ID`)
    from its Identity, output contract, and weights binding, rather than a
    dedicated shim class."""
    return Adapter(
        identity=Identity(name=_INTRINSIC_ADAPTER_NAME, adapter_type="{{ adapter_type }}"),
        io_contract=get_io_contract(_INTRINSIC_ADAPTER_NAME),
        weights=LocalFileBinding(
            name=_INTRINSIC_ADAPTER_NAME,
            adapter_type=AdapterType.{{ adapter_type | upper }},
            repo_id=_INTRINSIC_MODEL_ID,
            # A custom, non-catalog adapter has no catalog entry to resolve
            # revision=None from — pin it explicitly.
            revision="main",
        ),
    )


class {{ intrinsic_name_camelcase }}Intrinsic(Intrinsic, SimpleComponent):
    def __init__(self, {{ arglist }}):
        # adapter_types is required here: "{{ intrinsic_name }}" is outside
        # Mellea's intrinsics catalog, so Intrinsic has no catalog entry to
        # fall back to.
        Intrinsic.__init__(
            self,
            intrinsic_name=_INTRINSIC_ADAPTER_NAME,
            adapter_types=(AdapterType.{{ adapter_type | upper }},),
            # `_generate_from_intrinsic` never adds `action` to the context —
            # intrinsics reach the model only through their rewriter, driven
            # by `intrinsic_kwargs` — so these arguments must be passed here,
            # not just stored on the SimpleComponent side below.
            intrinsic_kwargs=dict({{ arglist_as_kwargs }}),
        )
        SimpleComponent.__init__(self, {{ arglist_as_kwargs }})

    def format_for_llm(self):
        return SimpleComponent.format_for_llm(self)

async def async_{{ intrinsic_name }}({{ arglist }}, ctx: Context, backend: Backend | AdapterMixin):
    # Backend.add_adapter should be idempotent, but we'll go ahead and check just in case.
    if _INTRINSIC_QUALIFIED_NAME not in backend.list_adapters():
        backend.add_adapter(_{{ intrinsic_name }}_adapter())
    action = {{ intrinsic_name_camelcase }}Intrinsic({{ arglist_without_type_annotations }})
    mot, ctx = await backend.generate_from_context(action, ctx)
    return mot, ctx


def {{ intrinsic_name }}({{ arglist }}, ctx: Context, backend: Backend | AdapterMixin):
    # Backend.add_adapter should be idempotent, but we'll go ahead and check just in case.
    if _INTRINSIC_QUALIFIED_NAME not in backend.list_adapters():
        backend.add_adapter(_{{ intrinsic_name }}_adapter())
    action = {{ intrinsic_name_camelcase }}Intrinsic({{ arglist_without_type_annotations }})
    return mfuncs.act(action, ctx, backend)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    from mellea.backends.huggingface import LocalHFBackend
    from mellea.backends.model_ids import IBM_GRANITE_4_1_3B
    from mellea.stdlib.context import ChatContext
    backend = LocalHFBackend(IBM_GRANITE_4_1_3B)
    result, ctx = {{ intrinsic_name }}({{ example_call_kwargs }}, ctx=ChatContext(), backend=backend)
    print(result.value)
```
{% else %}
This repository uses the **flat** adapter layout: the trained weights are stored
at the repository root.

> **Mellea adapter functions:** Mellea's `LocalFileBinding` and adapter-function
> loading expect weights at `<adapter-name>/<base-model>/<lora|alora>/`, so they
> cannot load this repository as an intrinsic. To use this adapter as a Mellea
> adapter function, re-upload it with
> `m alora upload ... --intrinsic --io-yaml ./io.yaml`, then regenerate this
> README with `m alora add-readme`.

{% if adapter_type == "alora" -%}
This is an activated LoRA (aLoRA) adapter. It only activates from its invocation
string onward, so the prompt must contain that string, after the input, the same
way it did in training.
{%- elif adapter_type == "lora" -%}
This is a LoRA adapter. It was trained on prompts of one fixed shape, so use the
same shape at inference.
{%- else -%}
The adapter was trained on prompts of one fixed shape, ending in an invocation
string, so use the same shape at inference.
{%- endif %}

Load it with PEFT, build the prompt, and decode only the generated reply:

```python
from peft import AutoPeftModelForCausalLM
from transformers import AutoTokenizer

model = AutoPeftModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained("{{ userid }}/{{ intrinsic_name }}")
tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("{{ base_model }}")

# Training formatted every input as: the item, a "Requirement:" line, then the
# invocation string.
INVOCATION_PROMPT = {{ invocation_prompt_literal }}
item = {{ example_item_literal }}
prompt = f"{item}\nRequirement: <|end_of_text|>\n{INVOCATION_PROMPT}"

inputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors="pt", add_special_tokens=False)
output = model.generate(**inputs, max_new_tokens=32)
print(tokenizer.decode(output[0][inputs["input_ids"].shape[1]:], skip_special_tokens=True))
```
